od or bad  worth saving or  not worth saving all based on his  personal impression of them  itadori and his sister are good people  no matter what they do  the prisoner yuji wanted to save or all  the curses  bad guys and honestly it's not really  hard to see  why he would think that way having been  abandoned by both parents and unable to  help his sister giving her condition  megumi doesn't really know how to  survive in the world  but pretends that he does because no one  else will take care of him  and on top of that you can add the fact  that he was basically forced into  jiu-jitsu first by his dad  selling him and then by gojo intervening  just to ask him to come work with him  instead  and since he actually is an incredibly  kind and caring person at heart  this simplified way of looking at people  helps him protect himself from people  leaving him again  as a result fushigoro tells itadori that  he might end up saving people who will  kill someone in the future  the irony is that foshiguro has of  course done this as well  saving itadore has led to more death and  suffering  and the same might be true for other  people he deems to be good  in fact he and itadori have a quite  similar approach to the central moral  question of the story  what does it mean to live a good life  worth living for both  it's saving people however while yuji  thinks about this in terms of quantity  saving as many people as possible  fushigoro looks at it in terms of  quality who he thinks is worth saving  and yet despite this difference in  perspective and the apparent naruto  sasuke dynamic between them  the relationship between itadori and  foshigoro is not only a major twist on  the shonen troll but actually the most  positive aspect of the story  [Music]  megumi and yuji aren't rivals but  partners  they support each other and keep the  other from falling apart under their  respective darkness  and so despite their contrasting  personalities they are actually the  closest to ea